      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="675" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Glycolytic glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH) catalyzes the conversion of glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate with concomitant reduction of NAD+ to NADH. Among the seven GAPDH genes in the Arabidopsis genome, two are plastidic, GAPCp1 (At1g79530) and GAPCp2 (At1g16300). In GAPCp1 and GAPCp2 double mutants, the NAD+-dependent GAPDH activity was reduced for about 25% in plastid-enriched fractions, when compared to controls. The double mutant plants had arrested root development, a phenotype could be reversed by serine supplementation in the growth medium. It suggests that the in vivo function of the plastidic GAPDH genes is to supply the 3-phosphoglycerate substrate to the phosphorylated pathway of serine biosynthesis in the plastid.</Comment>
